.TH VIDTIME 1 "2026" "vidtime @VERSION@" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
vidtime \- print how long media files run, and their total
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B vidtime
[\fIOPTION\fR]... \fIFILE\fR...
.br
.B vidtime
[\fIOPTION\fR]... \fB\-r\fR \fIDIRECTORY\fR...
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B vidtime
reads the duration of each argument with
.BR ffprobe (1)
and prints it as \fBHH:MM:SS\fR, followed by the total of everything it
could read.
.PP
Arguments that are not readable media are reported on standard error and
skipped. The total is still printed for whatever did work, so one bad file
in a directory does not cost you the answer.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-r ", " \-\-recursive
Descend into directories and time every file inside. Without this, a
directory argument is reported as an error rather than silently ignored.
.TP
.BR \-t ", " \-\-total
Print only the total, on one line, with nothing else. Useful in scripts.
.TP
.BR \-s ", " \-\-seconds
Print raw seconds instead of \fBHH:MM:SS\fR.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-color
Never colourise output. Colour is disabled automatically when standard
output is not a terminal.
.TP
.BR \-V ", " \-\-version
Print the version and exit.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Show a usage summary and exit.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
At least one file was timed.
.TP
.B 1
Nothing could be timed.
.TP
.B 2
Bad usage, or
.B ffprobe
is not installed.
.SH EXAMPLES
Time one file:
.PP
.RS
.EX
vidtime lecture01.mp4
.EE
.RE
.PP
Time a whole course and print only the total:
.PP
.RS
.EX
vidtime \-t \-r ~/Videos/course
.EE
.RE
.PP
Feed the total to another program:
.PP
.RS
.EX
vidtime \-t \-s \-r ~/Videos | xargs \-I{} echo "{} seconds"
.EE
.RE
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR ffprobe (1),
.BR ffmpeg (1)
.SH AUTHOR
Written by @MAINTAINER@.
